<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The lighting-mode marks of the icon system (docs/design/handoff.md §"Shared components",
    docs/design/mockups.md mockups 2b and 2f).

    The law, verbatim: "16px grid, 1.5px stroke, square caps, geometry only", and for these keys
    specifically: "Lighting-mode marks draw the motion (solid, breathing, spectrum, wave bars,
    rings, off) rather than naming it."

    Five rules follow, and every key below obeys them:

    1. One key per `KinesisEdit.Core.Lighting.LightingMode` member, named `LightingMark<Mode>`.
       All sixteen exist, including the two that never reach a mode menu - `Disabled` is the
       menu's "Disable" row, `PitchBlack` is the reserved `[black]` token - because the picker
       renders from the enum and a missing mark would be a hole in the rail.
    2. The mark depicts the effect's *motion*, never its name. The caption beside it already
       reads "Wave"; the mark's job is to say what wave looks like on the board. What each one
       draws is taken from specs/07-lighting.md §3 ("Visual behavior") - see the per-key comments,
       which name the sentence they come from.
    3. Every mark is authored inside a 16x16 coordinate box, origin top-left, y down, and the
       painted result - stroke included - stays inside it. Draw with `Stretch="None"` at
       Width/Height `IconSize` (16), or let the rail scale the box as a whole; a per-mark
       `Stretch` of Uniform would re-centre each mark on its own ink and break the shared centre
       the set is aligned on.
    4. There is no colour here and there must never be one. A geometry is a shape; the call site
       names the token it is painted with. Mockup 2f draws these as filled colour/gradient
       swatches - a rainbow block for Spectrum, a two-stop gradient for Breathe - and that is
       deliberately *not* reproduced: a baked gradient cannot inherit a theme token, cannot be
       recoloured for the selected row, and reads as a colour sample rather than as a motion.
       We draw 2b's geometry archetypes instead and let the token carry the colour.
    5. Stroke thickness and caps are the consuming control's job, not the geometry's:
       `StrokeThickness="{StaticResource IconStrokeThickness}"` (1.5) and `StrokeLineCap="Square"`.
       Nothing is baked in, so one pen change restyles all sixteen.

    Fill vs stroke, per key (the whole contract in one table). The three FILL marks are the ones
    whose meaning *is* solidity - a stroked disc is a ring, and a ring already means something
    else in this set:

      LightingMarkDisabled     stroke
      LightingMarkFreestyle    FILL    - four painted cells; stroked they become empty outlines
      LightingMarkMonochrome   FILL    - the solid disc; stroked it collides with Breathe
      LightingMarkBreathe      FILL    - a filled core inside a filled ring, see its comment
      LightingMarkSpectrum     stroke
      LightingMarkWave         stroke
      LightingMarkFrozenWave   stroke
      LightingMarkReactive     stroke
      LightingMarkRipple       stroke
      LightingMarkFireball     stroke
      LightingMarkStarlight    stroke
      LightingMarkRebound      stroke
      LightingMarkLoop         stroke
      LightingMarkPulse        stroke
      LightingMarkRain         stroke
      LightingMarkPitchBlack   stroke

    The three FILL marks are still safe to *also* stroke (their subpaths are all closed), but a
    stroked Monochrome is a ring, not a solid, so the distinction is meaning, not tolerance.

    Sizing hints are not re-declared here: `IconSize` and `IconStrokeThickness` already live in
    Themes/Geometry.axaml.

    Geometry is theme-independent - a shape does not change when the OS flips to light - so this
    is a plain ResourceDictionary with no ThemeDictionaries, exactly like Geometry.axaml.
  -->

  <!-- ===== Off, and the deeper off ======================================================
       Two modes mean "nothing lit" and both must be readable at rail size, so they are put in
       different registers rather than being two shades of the same idea. -->

  <!-- Disable. Mockup 2b's "off" archetype, verbatim: a single short horizontal bar. This is the
       row the mode menu labels "Disable" and the one a user actually picks, so it keeps the
       archetype; Pitch Black gets the other shape. Nothing is written to the led file for it
       (specs/07-lighting.md §2.2), which is exactly what a minus sign says. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkDisabled">M 4,8 L 12,8</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Pitch Black: the reserved `[black]` token - a board explicitly painted black rather than a
       board left unlit. Drawn as the board outline struck through: the "void" register, not the
       hatch one. The design reserves hatching for "off, not black" on a *key*, so hatching this
       mark would say the opposite of what it is; the slashed square keeps that register free and
       is the more legible of the two at 22x18, where a 4px hatch pitch fills in. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkPitchBlack">M 3.5,3.5 L 12.5,3.5 L 12.5,12.5 L 3.5,12.5 Z M 11.5,4.5 L 4.5,11.5</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- ===== The static modes ============================================================= -->

  <!-- Freestyle: per-key colouring, no effect (§2.4). Four painted cells on a 2x2 grid - keys
       addressed one at a time. Filled, because the point is that each cell carries its own
       colour; as outlines they would read as empty sockets. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkFreestyle">M 2,2 L 7,2 L 7,7 L 2,7 Z M 9,2 L 14,2 L 14,7 L 9,7 Z M 2,9 L 7,9 L 7,14 L 2,14 Z M 9,9 L 14,9 L 14,14 L 9,14 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Monochrome: one colour across the whole board. Mockup 2b's "solid" archetype - a filled
       disc, radius 5.5 on (8,8). The simplest mark in the set, for the simplest mode. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkMonochrome">M 2.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 13.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 2.5,8 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- ===== The fades ==================================================================== -->

  <!-- Breathe: per-key colours fading in and out (§3). Mockup 2b's "breathing" archetype. The
       breath has to be a *silhouette*, not an opacity - opacity is a brush property and cannot
       be baked into a geometry - so this is a small filled core (r 2.5) inside a filled ring
       (r 7 down to 5.5, i.e. one pen width thick): the disc caught mid-breath, with the halo it
       expands into.

       Three subpaths, and the middle one is wound the opposite way (sweep flag 0) from the outer
       and inner ones (sweep flag 1). That makes the annulus come out right under *either* fill
       rule, so the mark cannot break if a call site sets `FillRule="NonZero"`. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkBreathe">M 1,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 15,8 A 7,7 0 1 1 1,8 Z M 2.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 0 13.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 0 2.5,8 Z M 5.5,8 A 2.5,2.5 0 1 1 10.5,8 A 2.5,2.5 0 1 1 5.5,8 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Pulse: the *whole board* fading in and out (§3), which is what separates it from Breathe -
       Pulse writes only a speed, it has no per-key colours. So it is Breathe's concentric idea
       drawn in the board's shape instead of a key's: the board outline with its core throbbing
       inside it. Rectangles, not circles, is the whole difference between the two marks, and it
       is the true one. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkPulse">M 3,3.5 L 13,3.5 A 1.5,1.5 0 0 1 14.5,5 L 14.5,11 A 1.5,1.5 0 0 1 13,12.5 L 3,12.5 A 1.5,1.5 0 0 1 1.5,11 L 1.5,5 A 1.5,1.5 0 0 1 3,3.5 Z M 6,6 L 10,6 A 1,1 0 0 1 11,7 L 11,9 A 1,1 0 0 1 10,10 L 6,10 A 1,1 0 0 1 5,9 L 5,7 A 1,1 0 0 1 6,6 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- ===== The travelling effects ======================================================= -->

  <!-- Spectrum: a board-wide hue cycle (§3), with no per-key shape at all. Mockup 2f draws it as
       a rainbow gradient; without gradients the honest reduction is *banding* - four bands of
       different lengths and offsets, stacked. Staggered rather than centred so it reads as a
       spectrum strip and not as centred text. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkSpectrum">M 3,2.75 L 13,2.75 M 1.5,6.25 L 14.5,6.25 M 2.5,9.75 L 11.5,9.75 M 4,13.25 L 14,13.25</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Wave: "scrolls a rainbow across the board" (§3). A travelling wave with an arrow on its
       leading edge.

       This is the one place the set departs from a 2b archetype. 2b lists "wave bars"; here the
       bar register is spent three times over (Spectrum's bands, Rain's columns, Disable's bar),
       so a fourth set of bars would be the least distinguishable mark in the rail. A curve says
       "wave" more directly, and it buys the Frozen Wave pair below: the same wave, sampled and
       stopped. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkWave">M 1,8 C 2.4,3.6 4.6,3.6 6,8 C 7.4,12.4 9.6,12.4 11,8 L 12.4,8 M 11.8,5.8 L 14,8 L 11.8,10.2</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Frozen Wave: a *static* per-LED rainbow (§2.3, edge-only on the TKO) - the wave stopped
       and held, one fixed colour per LED. So it is the Wave curve sampled into five discrete
       stripes whose centres trace it, with no arrow: the motion is gone, the shape remains. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkFrozenWave">M 2,6.25 L 2,9.75 M 5,2.75 L 5,6.25 M 8,6.25 L 8,9.75 M 11,9.75 L 11,13.25 M 14,6.25 L 14,9.75</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Loop: "sweeps in the chosen direction" (§3), continuously and without end - a direction
       plus a repeat. An open circuit with an arrowhead closing on its own start: the sweep that
       comes round again. Kept open (315 degrees) so its silhouette cannot be mistaken for the
       solid disc or the breathing ring. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkLoop">M 13.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 11.9,4.1 M 11.5,1.9 L 11.9,4.1 L 9.7,3.7</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Rebound: "bounces a bar" (§3) between the edges of the board - the one effect whose motion
       is two-way, which is also why its direction control is Horizontal/Vertical rather than four
       arrows. Two walls with a double-headed arrow between them: the travel and the two ends it
       turns at. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkRebound">M 1.75,3.5 L 1.75,12.5 M 14.25,3.5 L 14.25,12.5 M 5,8 L 11,8 M 6.5,6.2 L 5,8 L 6.5,9.8 M 9.5,6.2 L 11,8 L 9.5,9.8</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Fireball: "shoots across a row" (§3) - directional, and the only effect that travels as a
       single body. A comet: the head at the leading edge with its trail streaming behind it. The
       trail is what makes it a shot rather than a dot. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkFireball">M 1.4,8 L 7.4,8 M 2.8,5.4 L 7.2,6.3 M 2.8,10.6 L 7.2,9.7 M 9.2,8 A 2.4,2.4 0 1 1 14,8 A 2.4,2.4 0 1 1 9.2,8 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Rain: "drops random columns" (§3). Columns falling from the top edge of the board, caught
       at three different depths - the stagger is the motion. The rail across the top is what
       separates this from Frozen Wave's stripes: these fall from somewhere. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkRain">M 2,2 L 14,2 M 4,5 L 4,9.5 M 8,7 L 8,13.5 M 12,5.5 L 12,9</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- ===== The press-driven effects ===================================================== -->

  <!-- Reactive: "lights keys on key-press over the base color" (§3) - the only mode whose motion
       is caused by the user. A keystroke coming down onto the board, with the light it throws off
       on either side of the impact. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkReactive">M 8,3.5 L 8,9.5 M 5.8,7.3 L 8,9.5 L 10.2,7.3 M 2,12.5 L 14,12.5 M 3.9,10 L 2.6,8.7 M 12.1,10 L 13.4,8.7</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Ripple: "expands rings" (§3) from the pressed key. Mockup 2b's "rings" archetype, drawn as
       the point of origin plus two arcs spreading from it, not as full concentric circles - the
       origin is half the mode, and full circles would collide with Breathe's ring. The gap widens
       with each ring (0.9 to 3.6 to 6.9) because that is what expanding looks like, and because
       three evenly spaced arcs in a 16px box leave under a pixel between them: at rail size they
       merge into a smear. Two rings and a source is the most this box holds legibly. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkRipple">M 7.1,12.6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 8.9,12.6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 7.1,12.6 Z M 4.4,12.6 A 3.6,3.6 0 0 1 11.6,12.6 M 1.1,12.6 A 6.9,6.9 0 0 1 14.9,12.6</StreamGeometry>

  <!-- Starlight: "twinkles random keys" (§3) - token `[star]`, not `[starlight]`. Three sparkles
       of three sizes, scattered rather than arranged: the scatter and the size difference are
       the twinkle. Every arm relies on the square cap to end cleanly at this size. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="LightingMarkStarlight">M 5,2.3 L 5,8.7 M 1.8,5.5 L 8.2,5.5 M 12.7,2 L 12.7,5.2 M 11.1,3.6 L 14.3,3.6 M 11.8,8.4 L 11.8,13.2 M 9.4,10.8 L 14.2,10.8</StreamGeometry>
</ResourceDictionary>
